.TH WHYKEY 1 "October 2026" "whykey 1.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whykey \- explain which layer handles a keyboard shortcut
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whykey
.I combination
.RB \%[ --verbose "] \%[" --color\ auto|always|never "] \%[" --json "] \%[" --json-v2 "] \%[" --schema-version\ 1|2 ]
.br
.B whykey inspect
.I combination-or-sequence
.RB \%[ --pid\ pid "] \%[" --focused "] \%[" --instance\ id "] \%[" --verbose ]
.RB \%[ --color\ auto|always|never "] \%[" --json "] \%[" --json-v2 "] \%[" --schema-version\ 1|2 ]
.br
.B whykey listen
.RB \%[ --repeat "] \%[" --timeout\ seconds "] \%[" --count\ n "] \%[" --events\ all ]
.RB \%[ --suppress " | \%" --no-suppress " | \%" --pass-through ]
.RB \%[ --dry-run "] \%[" --explain-capture "] \%[" --terminal "] \%[" --evdev "] \%[" --device\ path ]
.RB \%[ --output\ path "] \%[" --verbose "] \%[" --color\ auto|always|never "] \%[" --json "] \%[" --json-v2 ]
.RB \%[ --schema-version\ 1|2 "] \%[" --ndjson ]
.br
.B whykey doctor
.RB \%[ --color\ auto|always|never "] \%[" --json "] \%[" --json-v2 "] \%[" --schema-version\ 1|2 ]
.br
.B whykey capabilities
.RB \%[ --all "] \%[" --color\ auto|always|never "] \%[" --json "] \%[" --json-v2 "] \%[" --schema-version\ 1|2 ]
.br
.B whykey bindings
.RB \%[ --key\ text "] \%[" --action\ text "] \%[" --source\ text "] \%[" --device\ text "] \%[" --submap\ text ]
.RB \%[ --color\ auto|always|never "] \%[" --json "] \%[" --json-v2 "] \%[" --schema-version\ 1|2 ]
.br
.B whykey conflicts
.RB \%[ --key\ text "] \%[" --action\ text "] \%[" --source\ text "] \%[" --device\ text "] \%[" --submap\ text ]
.RB \%[ --color\ auto|always|never "] \%[" --json "] \%[" --json-v2 "] \%[" --schema-version\ 1|2 ]
.br
.B whykey extension
.I program combination
.RB \%[ --color\ auto|always|never "] \%[" --json "] \%[" --json-v2 "] \%[" --schema-version\ 1|2 ]
.br
.B whykey replay
.I file
.RB \%[ --verbose "] \%[" --color\ auto|always|never "] \%[" --json "] \%[" --json-v2 "] \%[" --schema-version\ 1|2 ]
.br
.B whykey snapshot
.I combination
.RB \%[ --output\ path ]
.br
.B whykey diff
.I before after
.RB \%[ --color\ auto|always|never "] \%[" --json "] \%[" --json-v2 "] \%[" --schema-version\ 1|2 ]
.br
.B whykey shell-init
.RB { bash " | " zsh " | " fish }
.br
.B whykey completions
.RB { bash " | " zsh " | " fish }
.SH DESCRIPTION
Whykey is a Linux-only diagnostic utility. Non-Linux targets are not supported
and fail with an explicit build-time platform message.
.B whykey
inspects a key combination through the typed input chain: an optional
remapper, the selected compositor, one terminal layer, input-method context,
the TTY driver, an optional tmux, GNU Screen, or Zellij session, the
interactive application, and the shell.
It never edits configuration files and never executes the inspected
shortcut.
.B listen
on Hyprland installs a temporary, leased Lua hook (see
.BR listen ),
and compositor extension manifests and
.B whykey extension
run the programs the user configured or named.
.PP
Uncertainty is reported explicitly, with a text assessment or JSON
.B confidence
of configured, confirmed, or conditional. A captured key in
.B listen
in its default mode proves that the event reached the current terminal, but
cannot prove what a compositor binding that captured the key would have done.
.B listen --evdev
proves only that a physical input event was generated before compositor
processing.
.B doctor
also lists candidate keyboard event paths, device names, and read errors so
that input-group permission problems can be diagnosed without changing device
state. When evdev is unavailable it names the cause: no
.I /dev/input
(not-installed), no keyboard event devices (api-does-not-expose), or
keyboards or a
.I /dev/input
that cannot be read (permission-missing).
In an SSH session without local Hyprland IPC, Hyprland is reported as not
applicable and the remote terminal path remains a valid doctor target.
.PP
The desktop adapters also cover herbstluftwm, GNOME, KDE Plasma, Xfce,
Cinnamon, MATE, Niri, dwl, River, Wayfire, labwc, bspwm/sxhkd, Openbox, X11
xbindkeys, AwesomeWM, Qtile, and XMonad. Sway and i3 are read by parsing the
running configuration returned by
.B get_config
and matching the mode reported by
.BR get_binding_state .
KDE Plasma and the Niri, dwl, River, Wayfire, labwc, sxhkd, Openbox,
xbindkeys, AwesomeWM, Qtile, and XMonad adapters parse configuration files
only and cannot observe runtime activation. sxhkd has no include directive;
whykey reads
.BR SXHKD_CONFIG ,
otherwise the
.B -c
file and extra configuration files of the running sxhkd, otherwise the
default sxhkdrc. An i3
.B --release
binding is reported as consuming the key press, as i3's source shows; a Sway
one forwards the press.
Xfce is queried through the live
.B xfce4-keyboard-shortcuts
channel; Cinnamon and MATE are queried through their GSettings schemas. These
queries are read-only and only report accelerator values exposed by the active
session.
.PP
The doctor command also introspects KDE KGlobalAccel, xdg-desktop-portal
GlobalShortcuts, and GNOME Shell through bounded read-only D-Bus queries. It
reports service reachability and explicit API limits; it never registers a
shortcut or invents a binding list.
.PP
External integration commands use a two-second timeout and a one-megabyte
stdout/stderr limit. Set
.B WHYKEY_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_MS
or
.B WHYKEY_COMMAND_MAX_OUTPUT
to override those limits for one session.
Static inspections also share a ten-second wall-clock budget across all
layers and sequence steps; each command's timeout is cut to the remaining
budget, and no command starts once it is spent. Set
.B WHYKEY_DIAGNOSTIC_TIMEOUT_MS
to override it.
.PP
keyd, kanata, kmonad, input-remapper, and xremap configurations are
evaluated for the queried key. A
remap re-runs the later layers on the target key, and the report reads, for
example, "keyd remaps Caps Lock to Esc." A layer switch, macro, or command
consumes the key. Tap-hold, one-shot, application-specific, and other timing-
or state-dependent actions stay conditional, as does a configuration found
without a running process. An unreadable configuration is reported as
unavailable. Printable keys are mapped by their US position and stay
conditional unless
.B WHYKEY_XKB_LAYOUT
or
.B XKB_DEFAULT_LAYOUT
is us with no variant. kmonad and input-remapper rules stay conditional unless the captured key came from the configured
device.
.PP
Literal Hyprland Lua helper calls provide source evidence and can resolve a
literal modifier-insensitivity option; Lua is never executed. A
.B __lua
or plugin dispatcher consumes the key unless its callback returns
.BR pass_event ,
which whykey cannot observe. Bindings that
.B hyprctl binds -j
reports with an empty key take their key from the text
.B hyprctl binds
listing, so Lua-built workspace binds such as super+1 match exactly, then
from a literal Lua
.B code:
hint, and are otherwise reported as possible matches for their modifiers.
.B bindings
and
.B conflicts
show a
.B code:N
bind as its key in the active XKB layout, and literal
.B source
paths with * and ? globs are followed.
Same-key bindings in inactive submaps are listed as inactive,
while dynamic configuration, unknown application modes, and
device-specific physical origins remain conditional unless an evdev capture
provides a matching device and keycode; evdev keycodes are converted to the
XKB namespace exactly once, and the native Lua hook already reports XKB
keycodes. If the active layout is not exposed, Whykey does
not invent a US layout. Malformed individual
Hyprland binding entries are reported and lower confidence rather than
discarding valid entries.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI combination
Inspect a normalized combination such as
.BR ctrl+left ", " ctrl+z ", or " super+c .
Join modifiers and the key with
.BR + .
Modifiers are ctrl, alt, shift, super, hyper, caps, num, and altgr (Mod5,
also iso_level3_shift), plus mod2, mod3, and mod5. The key is a single
character, a named key such as return, left, comma, or f1, an XF86 media
key, or
.BR code:N .
.B meta-key
is not a modifier; a combination containing it exits with status 2.
An unknown key name exits with status 2 and suggests the closest valid name.
An altgr combination predicts the text of the active layout's third level
(fourth with shift), sent as UTF-8; a dead key is reported as starting
dead-key composition with no bytes sent (JSON v2 path entries carry its
.B dead_key
keysym). When the keymap or level is
unknown, the report says why the encoding is unknown.
.TP
.B inspect combination-or-sequence
Inspect one or more whitespace-separated combinations, such as
.B ctrl+x ctrl+e .
Each step keeps its own evidence and uncertainty, and prefix state carries
across steps: Readline multi-key bindings and prefix keys, tmux prefix and
.B switch-client -T
tables, and the GNU Screen command escape apply to the keys that follow.
A layer only receives the earlier keys that actually reached it. A bare key
is looked up only in tmux's root or active table. Use the separate
.B conflicts
command for enumerated binding collisions. Focused-window discovery is supported by Hyprland, Sway, and i3; other
desktops report the missing API.
.B --pid pid
selects the process whose ancestry is used for interactive-application
discovery. A missing process is reported as unavailable.
.TP
.B --instance id
selects a Hyprland instance explicitly when more than one instance is
running. The selection is scoped to this inspection and does not change the
user's environment.
.TP
.B listen
Capture the next key reaching the terminal or, when available, through native
compositor capture (Hyprland, or Sway IPC). On Hyprland,
.B listen
installs a temporary Lua hook: the default pass-through hook only observes,
and
.B --suppress
also activates a private submap and restores the previous submap before
reporting. Each hook holds a 5-second lease that
.B listen
renews; it is removed on exit, and Hyprland drops it if the lease expires.
Configuration files are never edited. Press Escape or Ctrl+C to exit.
With the Kitty keyboard protocol active (Kitty, Ghostty, foot, WezTerm),
Ctrl+Z is captured and explained like any other key. On legacy-encoding
terminals Ctrl+Z, like an external
.BR "kill -TSTP" ,
suspends the job: the terminal is restored and the hook removed before the
job stops, and capture re-arms when the job continues. Use
.B --repeat
to capture more than one event. Capture without suppression is the default;
.B --no-suppress
states it explicitly, and
.B --pass-through
is a compatibility alias for it.
.B --timeout seconds
sets a wall-clock deadline for the capture.
.B --count n
stops after n captured reports.
.B --events all
includes modifier-only and release events with native compositor or evdev
capture.
.TP
.B --suppress
Opt into temporarily suppressing native compositor shortcuts while capturing.
Only Hyprland supports suppression. When native suppression is unavailable
(including on Sway), the command fails with status 1 instead of falling back
to terminal capture. Combining it with
.BR --terminal ,
.BR --evdev ,
.BR --device ,
or
.B --no-suppress
is a usage error (status 2).
.TP
.B --no-suppress
Capture through the native compositor without suppressing its shortcut. This
is the default; the option states it explicitly.
.B --pass-through
is a compatibility alias.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Select a capture backend and policy without arming it.
.TP
.B --explain-capture
Print the compositor detection and capture-factory evidence.
.TP
.B listen --evdev [--device path]
On Linux, capture readable EV_KEY events from input devices before the
compositor. This mode is read-only and does not grab devices; without
.B --device
it scans
.IR /dev/input/event* .
.TP
.B snapshot combination [--output path]
Save a versioned static diagnostic snapshot for one key combination. The
snapshot records the redacted inspection conclusion and its context for later
replay and comparison, not the raw IPC or configuration inputs that adapter
analysis would need to run again offline; this
command does not capture or inject keyboard input. Snapshots redact shell
identity, private paths, and secret-bearing assignments mechanically. Inspect
a snapshot before sharing it. Use
.B replay
to render the saved snapshot offline.
.TP
.B diff before after [--json]
Compare two saved snapshots or reports without querying the current desktop.
The text form lists changed context, request, and route layers; the JSON form
emits a versioned list of before/after values.
.TP
.B doctor
Check read-only prerequisites and report which integrations are available.
It also reports detected keyd, kanata, kmonad, input-remapper, and xremap
processes or configuration files and their literal mappings; transformations
that cannot be observed remain conditional. For evdev it reports a cause
(not-installed, permission-missing, or api-does-not-expose), a detail, and a
matching next check.
.TP
.B capabilities
Report the versioned support matrix for the current binary and environment.
Text output shows applicable and available entries by default;
.B --all
lists the complete adapter inventory.
The JSON form distinguishes implemented capabilities from planned ones and
from integrations unavailable in the current session.
.TP
.B bindings
Enumerate effective bindings exposed by detected compositor adapters. The
versioned output marks the inventory incomplete when application, plugin,
inhibitor, or firmware state is not observable. Use
.B --key , --action ,
.B --source , --device , and
.B --submap
to filter the enumerated fields case-insensitively. Device and submap filters
match only entries whose adapter reported that metadata; missing metadata
never matches an active filter.
.TP
.B conflicts
Compare the enumerated bindings for duplicate actions in the same source,
key, and context. Precedence and runtime activation that cannot be observed
are reported as possible conflicts. The same
.B --key , --action ,
.B --source , --device , and
.B --submap
filters apply to the conflict list.
.TP
.BI extension\ program\ combination
Run one explicitly selected application extension through the versioned JSON
stdin/stdout protocol. The response must declare supported evidence
capabilities and a validated result layer. Unknown capability and status
values are rejected; oversized responses are covered by the normal
timeout/output limits; whykey never discovers extensions or sends
the investigated key to them.
.TP
.B extension program combination [--json]
Run an opt-in external diagnostic executable using the schema-v1 stdin/stdout
protocol. The program is isolated in its own process group with a two-second
timeout. First-party extensions include
.B extensions/whykey-nvim
(live Neovim runtime via RPC),
.B extensions/whykey-vscode
(user VS Code keybindings), and
.B extensions/whykey-emacs
(live Emacs keymap stack via emacsclient). They report an answer as
indeterminate when the part of the editor that could handle the key is not
inspected: VS Code default keybindings and unevaluated
.B when
clauses, Neovim built-in commands and
.B vim.on_key
handlers, and Emacs translation maps and hooks.
.TP
.B compositor extension manifests
Place a validated manifest in
.B ~/.config/whykey/adapters/
or set
.B WHYKEY_ADAPTER_DIR
to add conditional static bindings and optional focused-window PID evidence
for an otherwise unsupported compositor. Commands are argv arrays, bounded to
two seconds and one MiB. Tier 4 manifests never provide
.B listen
capture; native Hyprland and Sway, terminal, and evdev remain the capture transports.
.TP
.B replay file
Read and render one or more concatenated schema-v1 or schema-v2 JSON reports
saved from a previous inspection or capture. Replay is read-only: it does not open input
devices, execute actions, or inject keys. Files are limited to 16 MiB.
.TP
.B shell-init bash|zsh|fish
Print an optional shell wrapper that supplies live binding snapshots to
.B whykey .
Evaluate it in the current shell; it does not modify shell startup files.
Without it, Bash answers come from inputrc files and Readline defaults, and
Zsh and Fish answers come from literal configuration and common defaults.
Answers based on defaults are reported as unverified.
.TP
.B completions bash|zsh|fish
Print a completion script for the selected shell. The scripts are generated
from the same command and option table as
.BR --help ,
so each command completes only its own options.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --json
Emit a machine-readable report. In listen mode, diagnostics go to stderr and
one JSON document is emitted for each captured event.
.TP
.B --ndjson
Only valid with
.BR listen .
Emit one compact schema-v2 JSON record per captured event on stdout. This
implies
.BR --json ;
.TP
.B --schema-version 1|2
Select the JSON shape. Version 1 remains the default for compatibility;
version 2 is opt-in.
.TP
.B --json-v2
Shorthand for
.BR "--json --schema-version 2" .
Selecting two different schema versions, such as
.B --json-v2
with
.BR "--schema-version 1" ,
is a usage error.
.TP
.B --verbose
Show every route layer and all diagnostic evidence in human-readable reports.
By default only matching, consuming, unavailable, or uncertain layers are
shown, with the conclusion first, and raw events, probe bytes, encoding
internals, full modifier state, XKB candidates, keyboard inventory, inactive
submaps, and alternate keys are hidden; JSON output always keeps full
structured evidence.
.TP
.B --color auto|always|never
Control terminal styling of human-readable output. The default,
.BR auto ,
disables color when
.B NO_COLOR
is set, stdout is not a terminal, or
.BR TERM=dumb ;
.B always
and
.B never
override those checks. JSON, NDJSON, shell-init, and completion output are
always plain.
.TP
.B --all
List the complete adapter inventory with
.BR capabilities .
By default only applicable and available entries are shown.
.TP
.B --repeat
Keep listening after each captured event. Only valid with
.B listen .
.TP
.BI --timeout seconds
Stop waiting after the given positive number of seconds. The deadline is
shared by all reports in a repeated capture.
.TP
.BI --count n
Stop after the given positive number of captured reports. A count greater
than one implies repeated capture.
.TP
.B --events all
Include modifier-only and release events with native compositor or evdev
capture. Normal capture reports completed key presses.
.TP
.B --terminal
Force terminal-only capture of keys that reach the terminal.
.TP
.BI --output path
Write listen reports to a file.
.TP
.B --evdev
Use the Linux evdev pre-compositor capture backend. It requires read access
to keyboard event devices and is optional.
.TP
.BI --device path
Read one explicit Linux input event device. This implies
.BR --evdev .
.TP
.B --instance id
Select a Hyprland instance for
.B inspect
when automatic selection is ambiguous.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The inspection completed successfully. For
.B doctor,
all required prerequisites were available.
.TP
.B 1
An unavailable prerequisite or inspection error.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage or key combination, including an unknown key name,
two different JSON schema selections, and a
.B --suppress
combination that cannot suppress.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
whykey ctrl+left
whykey ctrl+z
whykey listen
whykey --json ctrl+left
whykey extension extensions/whykey-nvim ctrl+x
whykey extension extensions/whykey-vscode ctrl+x
whykey extension extensions/whykey-emacs ctrl+x
eval "$(whykey shell-init bash)"
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hyprctl (1),
.BR stty (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR screen (1),
.BR zellij (1),
.BR bash (1)
